      {
        "type": "doc_title",
        "level": 1,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "The Transistor: History, Physics and Materials"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-fe1f6f741c0dc2fbc810"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "A short technical overview prepared as a sample document for document parsing.",
            "styles": [
              "italic"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph_title",
        "level": 2,
        "is_numbered_style": false,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "1. History"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-6db0e8ee4c37ff31e0ac"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "The first working transistor, a "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "point-contact transistor",
            "styles": [
              "bold"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": ", was demonstrated in December 1947 at "
          },
          {
            "type": "hyperlink",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_Labs",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "Bell Labs",
                "styles": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": " by "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "John Bardeen",
            "styles": [
              "bold"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": " and "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Walter Brattain",
            "styles": [
              "bold"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": ". Their colleague "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "William Shockley",
            "styles": [
              "bold"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": " developed the theory of the more robust "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "bipolar junction transistor",
            "styles": [
              "italic"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": " in 1948. The three shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1956."
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "1",
            "styles": [
              "superscript"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "The transistor replaced the vacuum tube as the basic switching and amplifying element of electronics, making computers smaller, cheaper and far more reliable."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph_title",
        "level": 3,
        "is_numbered_style": false,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "1.1 Key milestones"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-6a5662424359a3fe4fdb"
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "content": "<table>  <thead> <tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th><th>Organisation</th><th>People</th></tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr><td>1947</td><td>Point-contact transistor demonstrated</td><td>Bell Labs</td><td>J. Bardeen, W. Brattain</td></tr> <tr><td>1948</td><td>Theory of the junction transistor</td><td>Bell Labs</td><td>W. Shockley</td></tr> <tr><td>1954</td><td>Commercial silicon transistors</td><td>Texas Instruments</td><td>G. Teal</td></tr> <tr><td>1958</td><td>First integrated circuit</td><td>Texas Instruments</td><td>J. Kilby</td></tr> <tr><td>1959</td><td>MOSFET invented</td><td>Bell Labs</td><td>M. Atalla, D. Kahng</td></tr> <tr><td>1959</td><td>Planar silicon integrated circuit</td><td>Fairchild Semiconductor</td><td>R. Noyce</td></tr> <tr><td>1965</td><td>Moore's law formulated</td><td>Fairchild Semiconductor</td><td>G. Moore</td></tr> </tbody> </table>"
      },
      {
        "type": "table_caption",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Table 1. Milestones in transistor technology"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph_title",
        "level": 2,
        "is_numbered_style": false,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "2. Device physics"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-de043ef78b713bcf8aae"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph_title",
        "level": 3,
        "is_numbered_style": false,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "2.1 The p–n junction"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-b35763de6c084b4078ce"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "The current through an ideal p–n junction diode is described by the "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Shockley diode equation",
            "styles": [
              "bold"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": ":"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "equation",
        "content": "I={I}_{S}({e}^{\\frac{{V}_{D}}{n{V}_{T}}}-1)"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "where:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "attribute": "unordered",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "I",
                "styles": [
                  "italic"
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "S",
                "styles": [
                  "subscript"
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": " — reverse saturation current;"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "V",
                "styles": [
                  "italic"
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "D",
                "styles": [
                  "subscript"
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": " — voltage across the diode;"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "n",
                "styles": [
                  "italic"
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": " — ideality factor, typically between 1 and 2;"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "V",
                "styles": [
                  "italic"
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "T",
                "styles": [
                  "subscript"
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": " — thermal voltage, given by "
              },
              {
                "type": "equation_inline",
                "content": "{V}_{T}=\\frac{kT}{q}\\approx 25.85\\text{mV}"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": " at "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "T",
                "styles": [
                  "italic"
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": " = 300 K."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph_title",
        "level": 3,
        "is_numbered_style": false,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "2.2 The MOSFET"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-ed54e6755f373033fb6e"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "In saturation ("
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "V",
            "styles": [
              "italic"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "DS",
            "styles": [
              "subscript"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": " ≥ "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "V",
            "styles": [
              "italic"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "GS",
            "styles": [
              "subscript"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": " − "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "V",
            "styles": [
              "italic"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "th",
            "styles": [
              "subscript"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "), the drain current of a long-channel n-type MOSFET follows the square law:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "equation",
        "content": "{I}_{D}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\mu }_{n}{C}_{ox}\\frac{W}{L}{({V}_{GS}-{V}_{th})}^{2}"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Shrinking the channel length "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "L",
            "styles": [
              "italic"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": " increases the current and switching speed, which is the physical basis of "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Moore's law",
            "styles": [
              "bold"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": ". In its simplest form, the number of transistors on a chip doubles every period "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "T",
            "styles": [
              "italic"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": ":"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "equation",
        "content": "N(t)={N}_{0}⋅{2}^{t/T}"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph_title",
        "level": 2,
        "is_numbered_style": false,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "3. Semiconductor materials"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-9e4a8fe50ced04698578"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Silicon dominates digital electronics, while compound semiconductors are used where higher electron mobility or a wider band gap is required."
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "2",
            "styles": [
              "superscript"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "content": "<table>  <thead> <tr> <th rowspan=\"2\">Group</th> <th rowspan=\"2\">Material</th> <th colspan=\"3\">Electrical properties</th> <th rowspan=\"2\">Typical applications</th> </tr> <tr> <th>Band gap, eV</th> <th>Electron mobility, cm²/(V·s)</th> <th>Breakdown field, MV/cm</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Elemental</td><td>Si</td><td>1.12</td><td>1400</td><td>0.3</td><td>CPUs, memory, solar cells</td></tr> <tr><td>Ge</td><td>0.66</td><td>3900</td><td>0.1</td><td>Early transistors, photodetectors</td></tr> <tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Compound</td><td>GaAs</td><td>1.42</td><td>8500</td><td>0.4</td><td>RF amplifiers, LEDs</td></tr> <tr><td>GaN</td><td>3.4</td><td>1000</td><td>3.3</td><td>Power electronics, blue LEDs</td></tr> <tr><td>4H-SiC</td><td>3.26</td><td>900</td><td>2.8</td><td>EV inverters, high-voltage switches</td></tr> </tbody> </table>"
      },
      {
        "type": "table_caption",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Table 2. Properties of common semiconductors at 300 K (approximate values)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "eV\n0\n1\n2\n3\nSi\nGe\nGaAs\nGaN\n4H-SiC"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Figure 1. Band gap of common semiconductors at 300 K. Blue: elemental, orange: compound."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph_title",
        "level": 2,
        "is_numbered_style": false,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "4. Computing a thermal voltage"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-a07b5096648033ecaa68"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "The thermal voltage from Section 2.1 can be computed directly from physical constants:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "content": "k = 1.380649e-23    # Boltzmann constant, J/K\nq = 1.602176634e-19 # elementary charge, C\nT = 300.0           # temperature, K\n\nv_t = k * T / q\nprint(f\"V_T = {v_t * 1e3:.2f} mV\")  # V_T = 25.85 mV\n",
        "guess_lang": "python"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph_title",
        "level": 2,
        "is_numbered_style": false,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "5. Summary"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-c66e5d8468c0876e3497"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "attribute": "ordered",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "The transistor was invented at Bell Labs in 1947–1948."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "Two main device families exist:"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "list",
            "attribute": "unordered",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "bipolar junction transistors (BJT), controlled by base current;"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "field-effect transistors (FET), controlled by gate voltage:"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "list",
                "attribute": "unordered",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "JFET;"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "MOSFET — the basis of modern CMOS logic."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "Material choice trades off mobility, band gap and cost."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "start": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph_title",
        "level": 3,
        "is_numbered_style": false,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Glossary"
          }
        ],
        "anchor": "html-f190afd8339a7788ab18"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Band gap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Energy difference between the top of the valence band and the bottom of the conduction band."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Mobility"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "How quickly a charge carrier moves through a material under an electric field."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "CMOS"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Complementary metal–oxide–semiconductor logic built from paired n-type and p-type MOSFETs."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "attribute": "ordered",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "The 1956 Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded “for their researches on semiconductors and their discovery of the transistor effect”. ↩"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "Mobility and breakdown values depend on doping and crystal quality; the table lists typical figures. ↩"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "start": 1
      }
    ]
